WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.329 --> 00:00:05.049
For decades, the ISS has been an icon of international
cooperation.

00:00:05.049 --> 00:00:08.900
Two bitter enemies were able to put aside
their differences and embark on a project

00:00:08.900 --> 00:00:10.639
that would better all of humanity.

00:00:10.639 --> 00:00:15.920
After 23 years though, the ISS is starting
to meet its natural end, and Russia feels

00:00:15.920 --> 00:00:19.980
that it’s time to ditch the ISS and build
their own national space station.

00:00:19.980 --> 00:00:24.510
So, here’s how Russia plans to withdraw
from the ISS and their progress on their next

00:00:24.510 --> 00:00:26.470
space station.

00:00:26.470 --> 00:00:30.630
Before we get into Russia’s new space station,
let’s first take a look at why Russia is

00:00:30.630 --> 00:00:34.760
leaving the ISS in the first place starting
with of course aging.

00:00:34.760 --> 00:00:39.680
The ISS was mostly designed within the 1990s
and the first ISS module was launched way

00:00:39.680 --> 00:00:41.440
back in 1998.

00:00:41.440 --> 00:00:47.700
So, at this point, astronauts at the ISS are
basically using 20 to 30 year old technology.

00:00:47.700 --> 00:00:51.120
Nasa claims that there’s still plenty of
research that can be completed with this technology

00:00:51.120 --> 00:00:55.530
itself, but there’s no question that new
modules with up to date technology would make

00:00:55.530 --> 00:00:57.550
the process much smoother.

00:00:57.550 --> 00:01:02.190
The technology is not the only component that’s
aging either, the overall body and exterior

00:01:02.190 --> 00:01:04.720
of the ISS is deteriorating as well.

00:01:04.720 --> 00:01:09.720
Space is an extremely unforgiving place with
large amounts of radiation, super cool temperatures,

00:01:09.720 --> 00:01:12.000
and a plethora of space debris.

00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:16.320
Scientists did design the ISS to withstand
all of the harsh forces found in space, but

00:01:16.320 --> 00:01:21.500
as the ISS ages, the likelihood of some component
giving in is much higher.

00:01:21.500 --> 00:01:25.870
Just a few years ago, astronauts aboard the
ISS noticed a drop in pressure.

00:01:25.870 --> 00:01:30.631
After a little digging, they found that there
was a small hole in the walls of the ISS.

00:01:30.631 --> 00:01:34.380
They did quickly fix the issue and no one
was hurt, but this incident makes it clear

00:01:34.380 --> 00:01:37.450
that serious problems can and have occurred.

00:01:37.450 --> 00:01:42.410
Russia actually points to safety being the
primary reason that they are leaving the ISS.

00:01:42.410 --> 00:01:47.410
Last month, the Deputy Prime Minister of Russia,
Yury Borisov, stated quote, “We can't risk

00:01:47.410 --> 00:01:49.420
the lives [of our cosmonauts].

00:01:49.420 --> 00:01:53.530
The structure and the metal [are] getting
old, [and] it can lead to irreversible consequences

00:01:53.530 --> 00:01:55.409
— to catastrophe."

00:01:55.409 --> 00:01:59.380
Another major consideration when it comes
to keeping the ISS running is funding.

00:01:59.380 --> 00:02:03.560
The ISS costs a massive $150 billion to construct.

00:02:03.560 --> 00:02:08.390
We don’t know how much money Russia spends
on the ISS every year, but we do know that

00:02:08.390 --> 00:02:12.800
Nasa spends $3 to $4 billion annually to keep
the ISS international.

00:02:12.800 --> 00:02:16.840
Assuming that Roscosmos spends a similar amount,
Russia is throwing at least a few billion

00:02:16.840 --> 00:02:19.140
dollars at the ISS every year.

00:02:19.140 --> 00:02:23.270
By implementing all of the experience Russia
has gained from operating the ISS, it’s

00:02:23.270 --> 00:02:26.650
likely that they can build a new space station
that’s much cheaper to run.

00:02:26.650 --> 00:02:30.670
Something else to consider is that Nasa just
recently stopped subsidizing Russia’s portion

00:02:30.670 --> 00:02:31.980
of the ISS.

00:02:31.980 --> 00:02:35.640
Since Nasa retired the space shuttle, they
had been relying on Soyuz rockets to send

00:02:35.640 --> 00:02:37.260
US astronauts to the ISS.

00:02:37.260 --> 00:02:44.090
It’s estimated that between 2011 and 2019,
Nasa spent a hefty $3.9 billion to fly US

00:02:44.090 --> 00:02:46.540
astronauts on Soyuz rockets.

00:02:46.540 --> 00:02:50.310
This revenue no doubt reduced Russia’s net
input into the ISS.

00:02:50.310 --> 00:02:55.090
But, starting last year, Nasa started to rely
on SpaceX for crewed ISS missions.

00:02:55.090 --> 00:03:00.380
Starliner also just completed its first fully
successful uncrewed ISS mission, so Nasa now

00:03:00.380 --> 00:03:03.660
has two different options to send astronauts
to the ISS.

00:03:03.660 --> 00:03:07.930
As a result, I don’t think they’re going
to be buying tickets on Soyuz anytime soon

00:03:07.930 --> 00:03:12.240
meaning that Russia has to fork over more
of their own money to keep the ISS running.

00:03:12.240 --> 00:03:16.580
And this simply doesn’t make sense for Russia
when both them and NASA are ready for the

00:03:16.580 --> 00:03:17.580
next step.

00:03:17.580 --> 00:03:22.050
You see, the ISS is not the end goal when
it comes to space exploration.

00:03:22.050 --> 00:03:26.830
The long term goal is to return to the moon
and get people to mars and so on and so forth.

00:03:26.830 --> 00:03:31.230
So, neither NASA nor Russia are interested
in spending the majority of their effort on

00:03:31.230 --> 00:03:32.599
the ISS.

00:03:32.599 --> 00:03:36.910
Nasa is looking to finally return humans to
the moon in 2024 and Russia is working on

00:03:36.910 --> 00:03:39.850
putting humans on the moon themselves by 2030.

00:03:39.850 --> 00:03:44.680
In the meantime, Nasa is planning to open
up the ISS to private astronauts and tourists.

00:03:44.680 --> 00:03:48.650
While I don’t think Russia has any objection
against this plan, I also don’t think they

00:03:48.650 --> 00:03:51.950
really care to spend manpower on supporting
the effort long term.

00:03:51.950 --> 00:03:56.349
So, those are some of the primary reasons
that Russia is looking to ditch the ISS.

00:03:56.349 --> 00:04:00.430
Fortunately, they’re not just pulling out
of the partnership without an ample warning

00:04:00.430 --> 00:04:01.430
time.

00:04:01.430 --> 00:04:05.510
Russia said that they will confirm or extend
their departure date in 2025 meaning that

00:04:05.510 --> 00:04:10.430
Russia has given the ISS partners a minimum
of 5 years to prepare for their departure.

00:04:10.430 --> 00:04:15.281
Whether they leave in 2025 or 2030 is really
just dependent on how well their own space

00:04:15.281 --> 00:04:16.690
station development goes.

00:04:16.690 --> 00:04:20.709
Russia started working on this new space station
in 2015, and they hope to have it ready for

00:04:20.709 --> 00:04:21.709
launch by 2025.

00:04:21.709 --> 00:04:25.990
If they are able to meet this deadline, the
next goal is to put the entire station in

00:04:25.990 --> 00:04:30.439
orbit by 2030 at which point Russia would
completely exit the ISS.

00:04:30.439 --> 00:04:34.020
Something that we should note though is that
Russia said this exact same thing 6 years

00:04:34.020 --> 00:04:36.499
ago with different dates.

00:04:36.499 --> 00:04:40.889
In 2015, Russia announced that they were going
to leave the ISS in 2020 and move into their

00:04:40.889 --> 00:04:43.009
own space station in the early 2020s.

00:04:43.009 --> 00:04:47.639
But clearly, that didn’t happen, so it's
very possible that these deadlines are pushed

00:04:47.639 --> 00:04:48.740
back once again.

00:04:48.740 --> 00:04:53.789
I do think that Russia is much more motivated
to transition now that Nasa is no longer dependent

00:04:53.789 --> 00:04:54.860
on them.

00:04:54.860 --> 00:05:00.130
However, space technology almost always takes
longer to develop than expected, so a more

00:05:00.130 --> 00:05:03.830
realistic timeframe may be completing the
space station by 2030 and putting it into

00:05:03.830 --> 00:05:05.150
orbit by 2035.

00:05:05.150 --> 00:05:09.559
At that rate, Russia would stay on the ISS
for another 15 years.

00:05:09.559 --> 00:05:15.110
Nasa is looking to deorbit the ISS in the
2030s or 2040s, so at that point, Russia may

00:05:15.110 --> 00:05:18.759
ironically end up sticking with the ISS till
the end of its life.

00:05:18.759 --> 00:05:21.490
What will happen to Russia’s ISS modules
though?

00:05:21.490 --> 00:05:25.809
Well, Russia was originally discussing disconnecting
the modules and connecting it to their upcoming

00:05:25.809 --> 00:05:27.159
space station.

00:05:27.159 --> 00:05:30.949
But given the age of the older modules, it’s
very possible that Russia simply retrieves

00:05:30.949 --> 00:05:35.490
some parts of them to be placed in museums
and lets nasa deorbit the rest of the ISS.

00:05:35.490 --> 00:05:40.120
Anyway, moving onto the new space station,
Russia is aiming for the new station to cost

00:05:40.120 --> 00:05:44.539
just $5 billion and it is being manufactured
by Energia corporation.

00:05:44.539 --> 00:05:48.499
The new space station is called the Russian
Orbital Service Station or ROSS.

00:05:48.499 --> 00:05:51.280
The station is expected to have 3 different
docking ports.

00:05:51.280 --> 00:05:54.939
1 for Soyuz crewed vehicles and 2 for Progress
cargo ships.

00:05:54.939 --> 00:05:58.740
As for the core part of the station itself,
we have 5 different modules with the first

00:05:58.740 --> 00:06:00.469
being the airlock module.

00:06:00.469 --> 00:06:04.339
As the name suggests, this module acts as
an airlock for the space station.

00:06:04.339 --> 00:06:08.319
But more importantly, this module contains
the spacewalk hatch.

00:06:08.319 --> 00:06:10.689
Next up, we have the transformable module.

00:06:10.689 --> 00:06:15.309
This module is unique as it can expand and
constrict it’s internal volume.

00:06:15.309 --> 00:06:18.619
Once it’s attached to the space station,
there’s really no reason to constrict the

00:06:18.619 --> 00:06:23.039
volume of the module, but launching it in
its constricted orientation would save space

00:06:23.039 --> 00:06:24.410
on the launch vehicle.

00:06:24.410 --> 00:06:28.779
This module is expected to have exercise facilities
and likely sleeping and restroom facilities

00:06:28.779 --> 00:06:29.869
as well.

00:06:29.869 --> 00:06:34.059
As for the Prichal node module, this module
is supposed to serve as the core of the space

00:06:34.059 --> 00:06:36.900
station and connect all of the different modules
together.

00:06:36.900 --> 00:06:41.159
The space station is also slated to have a
gyroscopic module, but we don’t have details

00:06:41.159 --> 00:06:43.039
as to the purpose of the module.

00:06:43.039 --> 00:06:47.569
Anyway, the last two modules are all science
and research modules with the first being

00:06:47.569 --> 00:06:48.569
the Nauka module.

00:06:48.569 --> 00:06:53.149
The Nauka module was actually originally designed
to be docked with the international space

00:06:53.149 --> 00:06:57.929
station, but Russia decided to repurpose the
module to be used in their own space station.

00:06:57.929 --> 00:07:02.629
This module is officially a multi purpose
laboratory, but it also has a robotic arm,

00:07:02.629 --> 00:07:06.840
a science airlock, a radiator, and a couple
of orbital correction engines.

00:07:06.840 --> 00:07:10.659
Finally, we have the science and power module
which is apparently the most advanced portion

00:07:10.659 --> 00:07:12.219
of the space station.

00:07:12.219 --> 00:07:16.599
Not only will it have leading edge laboratories
and flight control systems, but it will also

00:07:16.599 --> 00:07:18.569
offer extra living quarters.

00:07:18.569 --> 00:07:22.319
Aside from all of these modules, the station
will of course have solar panels and propellant

00:07:22.319 --> 00:07:25.080
tanks to power the station and its engines.

00:07:25.080 --> 00:07:29.479
The entire station is estimated to have a
pressurized volume of 220 cubic meters.

00:07:29.479 --> 00:07:34.759
To put that in perspective, the ISS has a
pressurized volume of about 1000 cubic meters.

00:07:34.759 --> 00:07:39.639
The russian portion of the ISS is only 189
cubic meters though, so the ROSS is larger

00:07:39.639 --> 00:07:41.949
than the Russian part of the ISS.

00:07:41.949 --> 00:07:46.759
But in comparison to the overall ISS, the
russian space station is about 1/5th to 1/4th

00:07:46.759 --> 00:07:48.339
of the size of the ISS.

00:07:48.339 --> 00:07:51.669
Considering the smaller size of the station,
it is estimated that 2 cosmonauts will stay

00:07:51.669 --> 00:07:54.559
at the station for 2 month periods at a time.

00:07:54.559 --> 00:07:59.039
Russia plans to put the space station in a
lower orbit than the ISS in the 300 to 350

00:07:59.039 --> 00:08:01.029
km altitude range.

00:08:01.029 --> 00:08:04.589
Aside from being in a lower orbit, the space
station will also follow a different orbital

00:08:04.589 --> 00:08:05.910
path than the ISS.

00:08:05.910 --> 00:08:09.339
The Russians want to put their station in
polar orbit in order to monitor northern sea

00:08:09.339 --> 00:08:10.339
routes.

00:08:10.339 --> 00:08:14.029
They’re hoping to create new shipping routes
through the arctic as the arctic ice melts.

00:08:14.029 --> 00:08:18.369
On top of creating new arctic routes, Russia
is looking to get a much better view of mainland

00:08:18.369 --> 00:08:19.369
russia.

00:08:19.369 --> 00:08:23.849
Right now, Russia is only able to view about
20% of their territory from the ISS which

00:08:23.849 --> 00:08:26.749
has been one of their top complaints from
the very beginning.

00:08:26.749 --> 00:08:31.349
With polar orbit, Russia should be able to
view most of their territory unlike with the

00:08:31.349 --> 00:08:32.349
ISS.

00:08:32.349 --> 00:08:35.320
Aside from building their own space station
for the earth, Russia is also partnering with

00:08:35.320 --> 00:08:37.510
China to build a lunar space station.

00:08:37.510 --> 00:08:41.240
This actually sounds extremely similar to
Nasa’s lunar gateway project, but Russia

00:08:41.240 --> 00:08:44.550
isn’t really interested in participating
in the lunar gateway project.

00:08:44.550 --> 00:08:47.820
They say that the Lunar gateway is way too
US centric.

00:08:47.820 --> 00:08:51.590
They are, however, open to allowing other
nations to use their lunar space station once

00:08:51.590 --> 00:08:52.700
it is complete.

00:08:52.700 --> 00:08:56.210
But that’s pretty much all we know about
the russian chinese lunar space station at

00:08:56.210 --> 00:08:57.769
this point in time.

00:08:57.769 --> 00:09:03.250
At the end of the day, the ISS was a revolutionary
project both scientifically and diplomatically.

00:09:03.250 --> 00:09:07.810
As the ISS continues to age and space technology
continues to advance though, it makes less

00:09:07.810 --> 00:09:12.400
and less sense to support the ISS, especially
with larger projects around the corner.

00:09:12.400 --> 00:09:16.910
NASA themselves is looking to pull the plug
on the ISS within the next 10 to 20 years,

00:09:16.910 --> 00:09:19.740
but it looks like Russia isn’t willing to
wait that long.

00:09:19.740 --> 00:09:23.440
So, they’re basically putting in their 5
years notice as they look to build their own

00:09:23.440 --> 00:09:25.330
space station within this decade.

00:09:25.330 --> 00:09:30.090
The station isn’t nearly as large as the
ISS, but it is expected to be significantly

00:09:30.090 --> 00:09:33.810
cheaper, more efficient, and much more useful
to the Russians.

00:09:33.810 --> 00:09:37.020
Aside from this, they’re also looking to
partner with China to build a lunar space

00:09:37.020 --> 00:09:40.590
station, but we’ll just have to wait and
see how that turns out.

00:09:40.590 --> 00:09:43.330
What do you guys think about Russia’s plan
to ditch the ISS?

00:09:43.330 --> 00:09:44.730
Comment that down below.

